---
import SaidDocsLayout from "../../../layouts/SaidDocsLayout.astro";
import SaidShot from "../../../components/SaidShot.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  headline: "The keyboard is the desk",
  description:
    "Every Said and Done flow without the mouse: one rule, a handful of keys, no modes.",
};
---

<SaidDocsLayout
  title="The keyboard is the desk — Said and Done guides"
  description="Every Said and Done flow without the mouse: moving between cards, opening them, filing them, finding them."
  section="guides"
  kicker="Guide 02"
  heading="The keyboard is the desk"
  dek="One rule, a handful of keys, no modes."
  slug="the-keyboard"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <h2 id="one-rule">The one rule</h2>
  <p>
    Said and Done has one keyboard law and it never bends: <strong
      >a key goes to the topmost screen, and nowhere else</strong
    >. If the screen on top doesn't claim it, the key is dropped — it never
    falls through to the board underneath, never triggers something you can't
    see. There are no global shortcuts. Not for quit, not for search, not for
    anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    This sounds strict until you feel it. Every keypress is predictable from
    what's in front of your eyes, the help screen only ever shows keys that work
    right now, and a focused text field simply eats the keys — no modes to fall
    out of, no wrong-mode disasters to recover from.
  </p>

  <h2 id="moving">Moving around</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>arrows</code> or <code>h j k l</code> — walk the cards and lanes.
    </li>
    <li><code>enter</code> or <code>o</code> — open the picked card.</li>
    <li><code>esc</code> — close the topmost screen, always.</li>
    <li><code>tab</code> — hop to the next lane, keeping your row feel.</li>
  </ul>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/board.png"
    alt="The board with the cursor picked on a card, the ticker naming live keys"
  />

  <h2 id="making">Making and moving</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>n</code> — a new card.</li>
    <li><code>e</code> — edit the picked card, title focused.</li>
    <li>
      <code>shift + h/l</code> — carry the picked card a lane left or right;
      <code>shift + j/k</code> moves it within its lane.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>r</code> — resolve: done, dropped, or not relevant. The record keeps which.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On the dossier face the same <code>enter</code> opens the card's case file instead
    — deciding keys on the board, understanding keys on the record.
  </p>

  <h2 id="finding">Finding</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>/</code> — the finder, every project answering; <code>?</code>
      keeps it to this project.
    </li>
    <li><code>p</code> — switch project; <code>b</code> — switch board.</li>
    <li><code>space h</code> — the key help, honest by construction.</li>
  </ul>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/finder.png"
    alt="The finder open over the board, exact and meaning hits together"
  />

  <p>
    If a key is shown on screen, it works. If it isn't, it doesn't. You can
    trust your hands to learn the desk in a day.
  </p>
</SaidDocsLayout>
